WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.536
This is the technical deep dive
for the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:08.600
Workbook's companion guide,
Attribute-Driven Migration to Entra ID.

4
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.997
It's for identity engineers, Entra
and Active Directory administrators,

5
00:00:13.997 --> 00:00:19.125
Intune and Azure platform owners, PKI
and credential teams, and I S S O's.

6
00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:20.587
The idea is simple.

7
00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:25.362
In Active Directory, where an object sat
in the tree decided what applied to it.

8
00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:29.175
In Entra ID, what the object
says about itself decides.

9
00:00:29.175 --> 00:00:34.113
So the migration is the work of making those
statements true, and keeping them true.

10
00:00:34.113 --> 00:00:37.544
Microsoft facts were
checked on October 29, 2026,

11
00:00:37.544 --> 00:00:42.350
and the program's own recommendations
are labeled design guidance.

12
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.960
Let's start with why.

13
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.097
Here's why this matters.

14
00:00:46.097 --> 00:00:48.531
The organizational units, or O U's,

15
00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:52.585
of an Active Directory forest
were never just folders.

16
00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:55.343
That one tree decided who
could manage an object,

17
00:00:55.343 --> 00:00:59.273
which policy reached it, which office
owned it and what it could touch.

18
00:00:59.273 --> 00:01:01.098
Entra ID has no tree.

19
00:01:01.098 --> 00:01:02.761
Its directory is flat.

20
00:01:02.761 --> 00:01:05.104
So all four of those
jobs have to move into

21
00:01:05.104 --> 00:01:07.723
attributes and the
mechanisms that read them.

22
00:01:07.723 --> 00:01:10.572
The figure shows the payoff
and the risk together.

23
00:01:10.572 --> 00:01:13.910
One HR-sourced stamp
drives group membership,

24
00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:17.598
delegation, policy, licensing
and drift detection.

25
00:01:17.598 --> 00:01:20.935
If the stamp is wrong, all
five are wrong at once.

26
00:01:20.935 --> 00:01:23.720
So, Module 1, the model.

27
00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.208
Module 1 is the model.

28
00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:29.771
Before anything moves, everyone
needs the same picture:

29
00:01:29.771 --> 00:01:34.495
what the O U tree was really doing, where
each of those jobs lands in Entra ID,

30
00:01:34.495 --> 00:01:36.896
and the nine stages that get you there,

31
00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:40.440
each with one decider and
a gate that says it's done.

32
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:45.078
This slide is the translation table,
and it's the heart of the model.

33
00:01:45.078 --> 00:01:47.128
Placement becomes attributes.

34
00:01:47.128 --> 00:01:50.214
Groups built from O U's
become dynamic groups.

35
00:01:50.214 --> 00:01:52.927
Delegation moves to administrative units.

36
00:01:52.927 --> 00:01:57.640
And Group Policy inheritance becomes
Conditional Access and Intune targeting.

37
00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.693
Microsoft documents each piece;

38
00:01:59.693 --> 00:02:04.028
the program's contribution is the
contract that binds them together.

39
00:02:04.028 --> 00:02:08.161
One rule on the right matters more
than it looks: rules read codes,

40
00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:13.152
never display names, because a display
name breaks the day someone retypes it.

41
00:02:13.152 --> 00:02:16.156
And some native fields can't
be read by rules at all,

42
00:02:16.156 --> 00:02:18.866
which is why the schema
carries coded copies.

43
00:02:18.866 --> 00:02:20.560
Next, the path.

44
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.698
Here's the whole path on one page,
laid out this way for accountability.

45
00:02:25.698 --> 00:02:29.236
Nine stages, from reading
the tree to correcting drift.

46
00:02:29.236 --> 00:02:33.063
Every stage has exactly one
decider, taken from the books'

47
00:02:33.063 --> 00:02:36.972
two responsibility matrices,
and a gate that says it's done.

48
00:02:36.972 --> 00:02:41.566
Identity engineering owns most of
the plumbing, application owners own

49
00:02:41.566 --> 00:02:46.235
their access rules, and the CISO owns
the controls and the drift findings.

50
00:02:46.235 --> 00:02:47.789
And the gates are the point:

51
00:02:47.789 --> 00:02:51.110
nothing grants production
access until it's been proved.

52
00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.070
Stage zero first.

53
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.590
Stage zero matters because the
target has to reproduce intent,

54
00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.070
not structure, and you can't
reproduce what you haven't read.

55
00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.808
It's entirely read-only.

56
00:03:02.808 --> 00:03:07.381
Capture each O U's placement and size,
its Group Policy links and inheritance,

57
00:03:07.381 --> 00:03:10.938
and especially the permissions
someone delegated by hand,

58
00:03:10.938 --> 00:03:13.770
because that's where the
real delegation lives.

59
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.970
Then decide every group's fate.

60
00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.032
Two traps.

61
00:03:17.032 --> 00:03:21.295
A policy linked at a site looks
unlinked if you only search O U's.

62
00:03:21.295 --> 00:03:24.386
And the protected-account
marker stays set after someone

63
00:03:24.386 --> 00:03:27.220
leaves a protected group,
so it overstates privilege.

64
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.270
That's the model.

65
00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.020
Now the attributes.

66
00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:34.170
Module 2 is sourcing and
proving the attributes.

67
00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.048
Every mechanism later in this
session reads these values,

68
00:03:38.048 --> 00:03:40.957
so this is where the
migration is won or lost.

69
00:03:40.957 --> 00:03:45.908
We'll cover who owns each fact,
Federal HR 2.0 as the coming source,

70
00:03:45.908 --> 00:03:50.192
how HR data reaches Entra ID,
the facts HR doesn't supply,

71
00:03:50.192 --> 00:03:54.920
the schema and its single
writer, and then proof and sync.

72
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.083
This is the most important
ownership decision in the program.

73
00:03:59.083 --> 00:04:02.095
Each fact has exactly
one authoritative system.

74
00:04:02.095 --> 00:04:05.045
HR owns who a worker
is and where they sit.

75
00:04:05.045 --> 00:04:07.730
The identity platform
owns how they prove it,

76
00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.270
and whether a request
is allowed right now.

77
00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.058
The card system owns the PIV credential.

78
00:04:13.058 --> 00:04:14.753
Everything else is a copy,

79
00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:18.708
and corrections flow back to
the source, never into the copy.

80
00:04:18.708 --> 00:04:21.082
The other decision is the person key.

81
00:04:21.082 --> 00:04:26.190
Make it a key the agency controls, with
the HR system's own number mapped to it,

82
00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.732
so changing HR systems
doesn't break every identity.

83
00:04:29.732 --> 00:04:31.460
And that change is coming.

84
00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.728
Federal HR 2.0 matters
because it's about to become

85
00:04:35.728 --> 00:04:39.247
the source for most federal
employee attributes.

86
00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:45.285
O M B and O P M are consolidating core
HR onto one government-wide platform.

87
00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:51.998
The contract went to Oracle in June 2026,
and most agencies move in fiscal 2027.

88
00:04:51.998 --> 00:04:54.472
Confirm that schedule
with the program office.

89
00:04:54.472 --> 00:04:56.733
Here's the highpoint: nothing from O P M

90
00:04:56.733 --> 00:04:59.797
specifies how that platform
feeds your directory.

91
00:04:59.797 --> 00:05:02.085
The Entra design is yours to make.

92
00:05:02.085 --> 00:05:05.435
The book's advice is to put the
identity feed in front of the

93
00:05:05.435 --> 00:05:09.490
steering committee early, because
that window closes at go-live.

94
00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.965
So how does the record
actually reach Entra ID?

95
00:05:12.965 --> 00:05:16.288
Oracle's platform has no
native Entra connector,

96
00:05:16.288 --> 00:05:19.527
so the path is A P I-driven
inbound provisioning.

97
00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:23.287
Middleware in your own boundary
validates each HR event,

98
00:05:23.287 --> 00:05:26.966
records its provenance, and
posts it to Microsoft Graph.

99
00:05:26.966 --> 00:05:29.165
Then it lands in one of two places.

100
00:05:29.165 --> 00:05:31.874
For most users today
that's Active Directory,

101
00:05:31.874 --> 00:05:34.803
which syncs up, and the
Entra copies are read-only.

102
00:05:34.803 --> 00:05:37.953
For cloud-managed users
it's Entra ID directly.

103
00:05:37.953 --> 00:05:40.740
The two converge population by population.

104
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.417
It's included with the P1 and P2 licenses,

105
00:05:43.417 --> 00:05:48.720
and it's been generally available in
the government cloud since June 2025.

106
00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.937
HR supplies most of the
person, but not everything,

107
00:05:52.937 --> 00:05:56.658
and every gap needs a named
writer or it becomes drift.

108
00:05:56.658 --> 00:06:01.885
Contractors aren't in the HR system, so
each of their facts gets its own authority,

109
00:06:01.885 --> 00:06:04.657
like the contract record
or the PIV system.

110
00:06:04.657 --> 00:06:09.233
Device attributes come from device
provisioning, never from the user's record.

111
00:06:09.233 --> 00:06:13.844
And where someone signs in from is
a sign-in signal, not an HR fact.

112
00:06:13.844 --> 00:06:17.562
On the right is the design
that survives the HR cutover:

113
00:06:17.562 --> 00:06:22.683
an agency-owned key, standard data
elements, and one door into the directory.

114
00:06:22.683 --> 00:06:26.340
That makes the switch a source
swap rather than a redesign.

115
00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.556
The schema is where names,
codes and slots get fixed,

116
00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.702
and every rule will depend on it.

117
00:06:32.702 --> 00:06:37.487
The chain on the left is the ownership
model: the standard defines the code,

118
00:06:37.487 --> 00:06:41.802
HR owns each person's value, and
one provisioning service writes it.

119
00:06:41.802 --> 00:06:44.698
Access targeting uses three coded slots:

120
00:06:44.698 --> 00:06:48.190
organization, worker type
and position category.

121
00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:52.368
Some native fields, like hire
date, are readable only in preview,

122
00:06:52.368 --> 00:06:54.778
so they stay out of production rules.

123
00:06:54.778 --> 00:06:58.403
The panel under the table is the
rule that holds it all together.

124
00:06:58.403 --> 00:07:01.078
The provisioning service
is the only writer.

125
00:07:01.078 --> 00:07:06.390
Remove the help desk and self-service
paths, and treat any other write as drift.

126
00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.228
Stage two is the one teams want to
skip, and it's the one you can't.

127
00:07:11.228 --> 00:07:14.262
Everything downstream trusts these values,

128
00:07:14.262 --> 00:07:18.691
so a bad HR record becomes bad
access faster than any ticket queue.

129
00:07:18.691 --> 00:07:22.046
Baseline HR against reality,
fix errors at the source,

130
00:07:22.046 --> 00:07:26.928
and match every enabled account to a
worker record before the first full sync,

131
00:07:26.928 --> 00:07:30.665
because that match is what
prevents duplicate identities.

132
00:07:30.665 --> 00:07:33.132
The threshold is the agency's own call;

133
00:07:33.132 --> 00:07:37.428
ninety-nine percent on the pilot is
a starting proposal, not a rule.

134
00:07:37.428 --> 00:07:40.802
And expect a burst of serious
findings in the first month.

135
00:07:40.802 --> 00:07:43.827
That's the measurement working,
not the program failing.

136
00:07:43.827 --> 00:07:45.380
Then, sync.

137
00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:48.734
Stage three is the plumbing between HR,

138
00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:53.942
Active Directory and Entra ID while they
coexist, and failures here are quiet.

139
00:07:53.942 --> 00:07:58.159
The default sync engine is Cloud Sync
unless you have a Connect-only need,

140
00:07:58.159 --> 00:08:00.267
with password hash sync everywhere.

141
00:08:00.267 --> 00:08:03.176
Then map the dates and
the transitional fields,

142
00:08:03.176 --> 00:08:06.830
and run in shadow mode until
every difference is explained.

143
00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:07.955
Three traps.

144
00:08:07.955 --> 00:08:13.130
A soft or hard match can overwrite cloud
values with stale on-premises ones.

145
00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:17.630
Older Connect Sync servers
stop syncing in April 2027.

146
00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:21.236
And a provisioning record with a
missing field can clear a value,

147
00:08:21.236 --> 00:08:23.005
so always send the whole record.

148
00:08:23.005 --> 00:08:25.020
Now, the mechanisms.

149
00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.757
Module 3 maps attributes to mechanisms.

150
00:08:28.757 --> 00:08:33.389
The values are true and in place, and
now each replacement for an O U job

151
00:08:33.389 --> 00:08:38.833
reads them: dynamic groups, administrative
units, Conditional Access and Intune.

152
00:08:38.833 --> 00:08:41.048
Then everything else the tree held,

153
00:08:41.048 --> 00:08:44.408
meaning devices, Arc servers
and service principals.

154
00:08:44.408 --> 00:08:50.040
And finally PIV and CAC certificate binding,
which turns out to be an attribute too.

155
00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.527
Stage four replaces walking the
tree with evaluating a rule.

156
00:08:54.527 --> 00:08:57.562
Instead of asking who sits
under the Finance branch,

157
00:08:57.562 --> 00:08:59.678
you ask who carries the Finance code.

158
00:08:59.678 --> 00:09:04.583
Every governed group uses one of two
patterns: a node query on one organization

159
00:09:04.583 --> 00:09:09.866
code, or a functional query that crosses
organization with something like position.

160
00:09:09.866 --> 00:09:15.390
And every access rule explicitly leaves
out disabled accounts, guests and agents.

161
00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:18.273
Convert only the groups
whose membership really

162
00:09:18.273 --> 00:09:21.015
follows the organization,
and flatten nesting.

163
00:09:21.015 --> 00:09:24.886
The gate is strict: validate,
shadow against the old group,

164
00:09:24.886 --> 00:09:28.427
and pilot before any rule
grants production access.

165
00:09:28.427 --> 00:09:31.190
Next, who gets to manage these people.

166
00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.187
Stage five moves delegation,
and it matters because this

167
00:09:35.187 --> 00:09:38.365
is where help desks and
administrators get their reach.

168
00:09:38.365 --> 00:09:42.476
In Entra ID, a role is assigned
over an administrative unit,

169
00:09:42.476 --> 00:09:45.303
so it applies only to that unit's members.

170
00:09:45.303 --> 00:09:49.183
The figure is the three-tier
model: readers across the tenant,

171
00:09:49.183 --> 00:09:51.977
then department units,
then division units.

172
00:09:51.977 --> 00:09:55.121
Decide restriction for each
unit before it exists,

173
00:09:55.121 --> 00:09:58.339
with a default of no, because
you can't change it later.

174
00:09:58.339 --> 00:09:59.727
And remember the callout.

175
00:09:59.727 --> 00:10:03.202
A unit limits who can change
things, not who can see them.

176
00:10:03.202 --> 00:10:04.780
Then, policy.

177
00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:08.342
Stage six retires Group
Policy inheritance,

178
00:10:08.342 --> 00:10:12.293
and the risk is policies that
look strict but restrict nothing.

179
00:10:12.293 --> 00:10:16.158
Conditional Access targets
personas built from attributes;

180
00:10:16.158 --> 00:10:20.554
employees and contractors, for example,
come from the worker type code.

181
00:10:20.554 --> 00:10:24.664
The figure is the key pattern: a
grant policy on its own doesn't

182
00:10:24.664 --> 00:10:28.855
stop anyone outside the group, so
pair every grant with a block.

183
00:10:28.855 --> 00:10:33.017
Run everything in report-only
first, then enforce in waves.

184
00:10:33.017 --> 00:10:38.781
And watch for empty attributes, because a
dynamic group silently misses those users.

185
00:10:38.781 --> 00:10:43.410
Devices are the other half, and the
guide corrects the emphasis there.

186
00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.485
This slide sets the default for Intune
targeting, so it's worth getting right.

187
00:10:48.485 --> 00:10:52.035
By default each policy goes
to a dynamic device group.

188
00:10:52.035 --> 00:10:55.308
Microsoft's current
guidance, checked October 29,

189
00:10:55.308 --> 00:10:59.061
is to prefer assignment filters
where they meet the need,

190
00:10:59.061 --> 00:11:04.410
because dynamic device groups add processing
delay, and Book 05 now says the same.

191
00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:08.798
Send user settings to user groups
and device settings to device groups.

192
00:11:08.798 --> 00:11:12.077
The exception is tier and
role: filters can't read

193
00:11:12.077 --> 00:11:16.011
device extension attributes,
so those stay in group rules.

194
00:11:16.011 --> 00:11:20.710
And underneath it all, a device rule
can never read its owner's attributes.

195
00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:23.650
Next, how device attributes get written.

196
00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:29.062
Devices are attribute-driven too, but
only through their own attributes.

197
00:11:29.062 --> 00:11:34.243
Dynamic device groups read things like
ownership, trust type and enrollment profile,

198
00:11:34.243 --> 00:11:37.526
and Autopilot devices carry
markers a rule can select.

199
00:11:37.526 --> 00:11:40.925
Filters read Intune's own
inventory at check-in.

200
00:11:40.925 --> 00:11:42.900
The highpoint is the red panel.

201
00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:47.157
Writing a device's extension attributes
needs the right Graph permission,

202
00:11:47.157 --> 00:11:50.110
and it depends on the call:
a signed-in administrator

203
00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:53.062
uses one permission, an
unattended service another.

204
00:11:53.062 --> 00:11:56.612
Book 05's sample now
uses the signed-in one.

205
00:11:56.612 --> 00:11:58.390
Then, servers.

206
00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:01.459
Arc servers matter because they're the

207
00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:04.140
on-premises servers the
O U tree used to scope.

208
00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:09.552
Once connected, a server becomes an Azure
resource, and tags become its attributes.

209
00:12:09.552 --> 00:12:13.452
You set them at onboarding or
later, Azure Policy can test them

210
00:12:13.452 --> 00:12:17.428
and even inherit them, and Resource
Graph queries them at scale.

211
00:12:17.428 --> 00:12:19.365
One boundary to state plainly.

212
00:12:19.365 --> 00:12:24.047
Azure's attribute-based access conditions
apply only to storage data today,

213
00:12:24.047 --> 00:12:27.540
so Microsoft doesn't document
them for managing servers.

214
00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:30.127
Role assignments at the
right scope do that job.

215
00:12:30.127 --> 00:12:32.150
Next, applications.

216
00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:37.775
Applications and service principals are the
partial answer, and the table shows why.

217
00:12:37.775 --> 00:12:41.350
Each attribute store is readable
by different mechanisms.

218
00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:44.400
Extension attributes drive dynamic groups.

219
00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.347
Custom security attributes can't, but
Conditional Access can filter applications

220
00:12:49.347 --> 00:12:53.863
by them, and they suit sensitive values
that only specific roles should read.

221
00:12:53.863 --> 00:12:58.311
For service principals, workload
Conditional Access only blocks,

222
00:12:58.311 --> 00:13:03.487
applies only to direct assignment, and
needs the Workload I D Premium license.

223
00:13:03.487 --> 00:13:07.787
And Microsoft documents no dynamic
groups of service principals at all.

224
00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:11.138
So access codes stay in
the user extension slots.

225
00:13:11.138 --> 00:13:13.470
Here's everything on one page.

226
00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:18.508
This crosswalk is the reference you'll
come back to: every object type,

227
00:13:18.508 --> 00:13:22.720
the store its attributes live in,
who writes them and what reads them.

228
00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:26.133
Users, devices, Arc servers
and service principals

229
00:13:26.133 --> 00:13:29.545
each have their own stores
and their own consumers.

230
00:13:29.545 --> 00:13:31.520
The highpoint is the third column.

231
00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:35.225
Every row names one writer,
whether that's HR provisioning,

232
00:13:35.225 --> 00:13:39.470
device registration, the Arc agent
or an attribute administrator.

233
00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:42.839
That's the same single-writer
rule we set for the user schema,

234
00:13:42.839 --> 00:13:44.145
applied to everything.

235
00:13:44.145 --> 00:13:47.710
And one user row leads straight into PIV.

236
00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:53.285
PIV and CAC sign-in is attribute-driven
too, which is easy to miss.

237
00:13:53.285 --> 00:13:56.107
Entra ID finds the user
by matching a field

238
00:13:56.107 --> 00:13:59.010
on the certificate to
a field on the account.

239
00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:02.592
Some matches are low affinity,
like the name on the card,

240
00:14:02.592 --> 00:14:05.935
and some are high, tied to
one specific certificate.

241
00:14:05.935 --> 00:14:07.098
Two highpoints.

242
00:14:07.098 --> 00:14:11.730
Certificate-based sign-in is only as
strong as the weakest binding you allow,

243
00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:15.260
and the program's position is
high affinity in production.

244
00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:18.007
But switch the whole tenant
to high before every

245
00:14:18.007 --> 00:14:20.685
account's value is right,
and you lock people out.

246
00:14:20.685 --> 00:14:21.885
Pilot it first.

247
00:14:21.885 --> 00:14:24.060
So where does that value come from?

248
00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:28.707
The certificate user I Ds
attribute holds the binding,

249
00:14:28.707 --> 00:14:31.998
so who writes it matters
as much as any HR field.

250
00:14:31.998 --> 00:14:37.272
For cloud-only users, a privileged
authentication administrator or Graph writes it.

251
00:14:37.272 --> 00:14:41.608
For synced users, only Entra
Connect can, from Active Directory,

252
00:14:41.608 --> 00:14:44.860
and Microsoft's page
doesn't mention Cloud Sync.

253
00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:49.419
HR supplies the person; the card
management system supplies the binding,

254
00:14:49.419 --> 00:14:51.623
because a reissued card changes it.

255
00:14:51.623 --> 00:14:53.568
After Active Directory is gone,

256
00:14:53.568 --> 00:14:56.711
the plan is for the card
system to write it directly.

257
00:14:56.711 --> 00:15:00.123
Be clear that this is design
guidance, and unverified.

258
00:15:00.123 --> 00:15:03.447
It's an open pilot,
workbook issue one thirty.

259
00:15:03.447 --> 00:15:04.850
Now let's run it.

260
00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.750
Module 4 runs the migration.

261
00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:11.593
We'll follow one analyst through
a reassignment with no ticket,

262
00:15:11.593 --> 00:15:15.879
then cut over in phases, keep the
attributes true with drift detection,

263
00:15:15.879 --> 00:15:19.870
and finish with the limits, the
checklist and the decisions.

264
00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:24.595
Here's the model working on one
person, which is the best test of it.

265
00:15:24.595 --> 00:15:29.420
She's a budget analyst in a finance
division, still mastered in Active Directory.

266
00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:34.920
Her HR record becomes a handful of coded
attributes, and every mechanism reads them.

267
00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:39.795
Her organization code puts her in the right
groups, and in a database reader group.

268
00:15:39.795 --> 00:15:44.020
Her division puts her in the unit
whose help desk can reset her password.

269
00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:46.870
Her worker type puts her
in the employee persona.

270
00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:50.633
And her laptop is governed by
its own attributes, not hers.

271
00:15:50.633 --> 00:15:52.969
Nobody assigned any of it by hand.

272
00:15:52.969 --> 00:15:54.880
Now HR moves her.

273
00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:57.755
This is why the whole design exists.

274
00:15:57.755 --> 00:16:01.899
HR records her move to accounts
payable as a personnel action,

275
00:16:01.899 --> 00:16:03.605
and nobody opens a ticket.

276
00:16:03.605 --> 00:16:08.230
The record flows through the middleware
to Active Directory and up through sync.

277
00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.462
Her groups and units recompute,

278
00:16:10.462 --> 00:16:14.068
usually within hours, but it
can take more than a day.

279
00:16:14.068 --> 00:16:18.781
A workflow swaps her access packages
and starts a review of what's left.

280
00:16:18.781 --> 00:16:22.418
Her laptop stays put, because
device rules can't see her.

281
00:16:22.418 --> 00:16:25.567
And a drift scan checks
the result field by field.

282
00:16:25.567 --> 00:16:27.970
Next, cutting over at scale.

283
00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:34.132
Stage seven is a runbook, not a flag day,
and that's what keeps rollback possible.

284
00:16:34.132 --> 00:16:36.692
Waves are defined by attribute values,

285
00:16:36.692 --> 00:16:39.971
so each wave is a query
whose groups already exist.

286
00:16:39.971 --> 00:16:44.520
Start with a pilot division, and keep
each one drift-clean before the next.

287
00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:48.983
Do leaver automation first, because
revocation is where the risk is.

288
00:16:48.983 --> 00:16:52.632
The gate is the strip across
the top: a signed report,

289
00:16:52.632 --> 00:16:57.932
a dry run with writes off, then seven
days in which every change traces to HR.

290
00:16:57.932 --> 00:17:00.095
Only then does the ticket path close.

291
00:17:00.095 --> 00:17:03.733
And disable, never delete,
so you can always go back.

292
00:17:03.733 --> 00:17:05.190
Then, drift.

293
00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:09.563
Stage eight keeps the
statements true after cutover;

294
00:17:09.563 --> 00:17:12.115
without it, everything slowly decays.

295
00:17:12.115 --> 00:17:16.062
Two comparisons run on a schedule:
HR against the directory,

296
00:17:16.062 --> 00:17:19.365
and the unit and role
registry against the tenant.

297
00:17:19.365 --> 00:17:24.448
Every finding goes to the CISO and is
fixed at the source, in the HR record or

298
00:17:24.448 --> 00:17:29.777
the rule, never in the copy, because the
next cycle would just overwrite your fix.

299
00:17:29.777 --> 00:17:32.177
The callout is the one to act on today.

300
00:17:32.177 --> 00:17:35.007
Entra's logs don't last
nearly as long as federal

301
00:17:35.007 --> 00:17:38.053
records rules require, and
they aren't retroactive.

302
00:17:38.053 --> 00:17:39.541
Route them out from day one.

303
00:17:39.541 --> 00:17:41.350
Now, the limits.

304
00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:45.425
Every design has edges, and
these are the ones that bite.

305
00:17:45.425 --> 00:17:47.710
Privileged groups can't be dynamic,

306
00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:52.675
so privilege stays assigned and time-bound,
and HR decides only eligibility.

307
00:17:52.675 --> 00:17:57.037
Rules have size and count limits, and
they can't read some native fields.

308
00:17:57.037 --> 00:18:01.208
Then the date: the member-of
preview ends on November 3,

309
00:18:01.208 --> 00:18:04.525
2026, and groups that
use it freeze in place.

310
00:18:04.525 --> 00:18:08.250
Microsoft's page still
said so on October 29.

311
00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.915
And membership can lag by more than a day,

312
00:18:10.915 --> 00:18:14.550
so an urgent removal means
disable and revoke, not wait.

313
00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.240
More limits next.

314
00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:18.252
A few more limits.

315
00:18:18.252 --> 00:18:22.965
Administrative units don't nest, and
they limit changes, not visibility.

316
00:18:22.965 --> 00:18:26.884
Dynamic units work in G C C,
but not in G C C High or D O D.

317
00:18:26.884 --> 00:18:32.141
Lifecycle Workflows allow up to
one hundred workflows per tenant,

318
00:18:32.141 --> 00:18:35.678
and no built-in task
revokes a PIV credential.

319
00:18:35.678 --> 00:18:39.094
Active Directory stays
necessary during hybrid for

320
00:18:39.094 --> 00:18:42.665
synced users' attributes,
Kerberos apps and some policy.

321
00:18:42.665 --> 00:18:46.597
Moving a user's source of
authority has firm prerequisites,

322
00:18:46.597 --> 00:18:50.128
and whether it's available in
G C C is still unverified.

323
00:18:50.128 --> 00:18:51.820
Here's the checklist.

324
00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:56.460
The checklist turns the nine stages
into something you can tick off,

325
00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.057
and that's its whole purpose.

326
00:18:58.057 --> 00:19:01.357
There's one row per
stage, plus one for PIV.

327
00:19:01.357 --> 00:19:02.933
A few are easy to miss.

328
00:19:02.933 --> 00:19:05.644
Export site links, not just O U links.

329
00:19:05.644 --> 00:19:08.195
Match accounts before the first full sync.

330
00:19:08.195 --> 00:19:12.133
Write every access rule on
codes, and don't use member-of.

331
00:19:12.133 --> 00:19:15.245
Decide unit restriction
before the unit exists.

332
00:19:15.245 --> 00:19:17.283
Pair every grant with a block.

333
00:19:17.283 --> 00:19:21.532
Prove seven days of sole-source
changes before closing the ticket path.

334
00:19:21.532 --> 00:19:23.582
And route logs out from the first day.

335
00:19:23.582 --> 00:19:25.680
Finally, who decides.

336
00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:28.429
Every decision here has one owner,

337
00:19:28.429 --> 00:19:31.355
and that's what keeps the
migration from stalling.

338
00:19:31.355 --> 00:19:34.241
Identity engineering owns the plumbing,

339
00:19:34.241 --> 00:19:37.467
the delegation model and
each wave's go or no-go.

340
00:19:37.467 --> 00:19:41.793
The ICAM governance body owns the
authority map and the person key.

341
00:19:41.793 --> 00:19:45.061
HR owns the codes, the
data architect the schema,

342
00:19:45.061 --> 00:19:48.329
and application owners
their own access rules.

343
00:19:48.329 --> 00:19:51.730
The CISO owns the controls
and the drift findings.

344
00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:57.197
And you can start Monday without new tools:
export the tree, name the HR feed early,

345
00:19:57.197 --> 00:20:01.918
populate the person key, remove other
writers, and route your logs out.

346
00:20:01.918 --> 00:20:04.856
Make the attributes
true, and keep them true.

347
00:20:04.856 --> 00:20:05.670
Thank you.
